ective campaign encouraged people to call their local cable companies to demand that they carry the new channel, significantly expanding MTV's reach and power across the US. \"Promos like this began to run constantly on MTV featuring the police, Pat Benatar, Pete Townsend, Cindy Loper, Billy Eidle, and even David Bowie.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Rise and Fall and Future of the Music Video - Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lqd6IbUJ7tg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Age at Breakthrough","body":"When Cyndi Lauper broke into the pop scene in 1984, she was 30 years old, which was considered \"fairly mature for brand new pop stars\" at the time. Despite her age, she launched a highly successful career with an original pop persona. \"Loper 30 years old, fairly mature for brand new pop stars.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"What’s 1984 Got to Do with It Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"What’s 1984 Got to Do with It Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef5154465cd600c4fa3"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"First Woman with Four Top Five Hits from One Album","body":"By the end of 1984, Cyndi Lauper made history by becoming the first woman to achieve four top five hits from a single album, \"She's So Unusual.\" This remarkable accomplishment showcased her immediate and significant impact on the pop charts. \"By the end of 84, Loper became the first woman to pull four top five hits from a single album.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"What’s 1984 Got to Do with It Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"What’s 1984 Got to Do with It Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef5154465cd600c4fa3"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"MTV Video Music Award for Best Female Video (1984) — Girls Just Want to Have Fun","body":"Won.","year":1984,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1091031"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1985)","body":"Nominated.","year":1985,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1453643"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Guest Actress in a Comedy Series (1995) — Mad About You","body":"Won.","year":1995,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2981152"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best Musical Theater Album (2013) — Kinky Boots","body":"Won.","year":2013,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3113385"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Tony Award for Best Original Score (2013) — Kinky Boots","body":"Won.","year":2013,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1478089"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"VH1 Trailblazer Honors (2017)","body":"Won.","year":2017,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q55316905"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Tony Award for Best Original Score (2018) — The SpongeBob Musical","body":"Nominated.","year":2018,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1478089"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"star on Hollywood Walk of Fame","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17985761"}]}],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":256613398935010,"type":"album","name":"She’s So Unusual","work_year":1983,"list_id":192934750972716,"list":"1001 Albums You Must Hear Before You Die","src":"charts","rank":521,"year":1983,"no